    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d364">

                <pb facs="0333" n="323" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg323"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/5–349: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Taipei</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Edgar</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Taipei</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-05-03T11:00:00+09:00">May 3, 1949—11 a.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 3—4:58 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>139. From Merchant. RefDeptel 177, April 25 to Canton. Through Dr. Wu, who has
                    Government’s ear, Edgar and I have made clear belief further influx mainlanders
                    would prove political health and economic liability to Formosa.</p>
                <p>Relative aircraft arrivals recently tend indicate Canton and Hong Kong preferred
                    over Taipei as haven by Shanghai refugees whose wealth, position enable air
                    exit.</p>
                <p>Wu stated flatly no troops left Formosa for mainland; some but “not substantial
                    numbers” from Shanghai came and coming here. [Merchant.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Edgar</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
